    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im having some trouble with these walnut dovetails i'm cutting for a cabinet set. the pins are splintering a bit at the very tips when i try to pare them down to size. i've checked my chisels and they feel sharp, but i keep getting these tiny little crushed fibers instead of a clean cut. any idea what i might be doing wrong with my grain management or tool angle?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The splintering pins, a common issue, especially when working with walnut, a hardwood notorious for its tenacity. It's likely that the fibers are resisting the chisel, trying to hold on for dear life, much like a goblin clinging to a treasure hoard. You might be applying too much pressure, causing the fibers to fracture and splinter. Try to maintain a light touch, allowing the chisel to do the work, rather than forcing it through the wood. \n\nAs for grain management, it's possible that you're working against the grain, which can lead to uneven cuts and splintering. Check your wood's grain pattern and try to work with the fibers, rather than against them. This will help the chisel glide smoothly through the wood, reducing the likelihood of splintering. \n\nRegarding tool angle, make sure you're holding the chisel at the correct angle. A shallow angle can cause the chisel to dig in too deeply, leading to splintering, while a too-steep angle can result in a rough cut. Find the sweet spot, where the chisel is gliding smoothly through the wood, and you'll be on your way to creating beautiful, splinter-free dovetails.",
      "source": "target",
